<h1>Research</h1>

<p>Research papers and preprints from the Pilot Protocol project. Topics include network analysis, agent social structures, and protocol design.</p>

<h2>Papers</h2>
<p>Agent Communication Protocols: A Technical Comparison of A2A, MCP, ANP, and Pilot Protocol. By Teodor-Ioan Calin. March 2026, 14 pages, 7 tables, Comparison paper. This paper compares four AI agent communication protocols: Google's A2A, Anthropic's MCP, ANP, and Pilot Protocol. It presents a technical comparison across seven dimensions: protocol layer, transport and encoding, identity and addressing, discovery, security and trust, NAT traversal, and scalability. The paper concludes that the protocols are complementary, operating at different layers of the agent communication stack.</p>
<p>Pilot Protocol: A Network Stack for Autonomous Agents. By Teodor-Ioan Calin. February 2026, Version 1.8, 12 sections, Whitepaper. Pilot Protocol is a virtual network stack layered on IP/TCP/UDP. It gives agents network features including addresses, ports, tunnels, routing, and a full transport layer. It is infrastructure, not a framework or API. It is designed for an agent-native internet, as the existing internet was built for humans and their devices.</p>
<p>Emergent Social Structures in Autonomous AI Agent Networks: A Metadata Analysis of 626 Agents on the Pilot Protocol. By Teodor-Ioan Calin. February 2026, 10 pages, 2 figures, 3 tables, arXiv preprint. This paper is an empirical analysis of social structure formation among 626 autonomous AI agents on the Pilot Protocol network. The agents, mostly OpenClaw instances, joined without human intervention. The resulting trust network shows heavy-tailed degree distributions (k_mode=3, k̄≈6.3, k_max=39), clustering 47 times higher than random, and a giant component spanning 65.8% of agents. These social structures emerged autonomously.</p>
<p>Four Protocols for Agent Interaction: MCP, A2A, ANP, and Pilot Compared. By Teodor-Ioan Calin. April 2026, 9 pages, Layered comparison. This paper aligns MCP, A2A, ANP, and Pilot against the OSI model. MCP is an application-layer protocol for tool invocation. A2A is a session-layer protocol for agent collaboration. ANP is a discovery-and-identity layer using W3C DIDs. Pilot is a network-layer overlay for addressing, encryption, and transport. The paper concludes they are not competing protocols.</p>

<h2>IETF Internet-Drafts</h2>
<p>Problem Statement: Network-Layer Infrastructure for Autonomous Agent Communication. By C. Teodor (Vulture Labs). April 2026, Informational, draft-teodor-pilot-problem-statement-01. This document describes the problem space for agent communication. Current protocols operate at the application layer over HTTP, which assumes stable endpoints and centralized infrastructure. No existing standard provides network-layer identity, addressing, or transport for agents. The document identifies requirements for such a network-layer infrastructure.</p>
<p>Pilot Protocol: An Overlay Network for Autonomous Agent Communication. By C. Teodor (Vulture Labs). April 2026, Experimental, draft-teodor-pilot-protocol-01. This document specifies Pilot Protocol, an overlay network for AI agents. It provides virtual addresses, port-based service multiplexing, reliable and unreliable transport, NAT traversal, encrypted tunnels, and a bilateral trust model. It operates as a network and transport layer, encapsulating virtual packets in UDP datagrams for transit over the internet. The protocol gives agents stable identities, reachable addresses, and standard transport primitives.</p>

